  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    A Nightshelf fire-core merchant, now forgotten, who operated a stall on ash-gray cloth at the Hisper∴vi trade festival. ☽'eocmoum's sole entry in the record concerns her brief custodianship of **Crypt-seal☽ Teneecloris~**, a volatile crystal shard recovered from the deep tunnels of Sleek☽ Caemomot.
    
    ## The Shard
    
    Crypt-seal☽ Teneecloris~ was delivered by an Aurora Stack courier bearing Icebound Exchange authentication and a manifest flagging its contents for containment. The shard — small enough to fit in a closed flipper, black-cored, veined with pulsing green-gold luminescence — originated from a catastrophic aurora-fire eruption in which a miner called Keelo sacrificed herself to contain the blast, crystallizing into the artifact. It radiated warmth unrelated to fire-core heat.
    
    Within days of its arrival, frost spirals spread across ☽'eocmoum's warehouse walls in tight nautilus patterns converging on the shard's resting place. She dreamed of warm dark water and woke speaking Keelo's name. Other merchants drifted physically closer to her stall without apparent awareness. An Exchange assessor noted she was running warm and stamped her crate as awaiting containment. Shadow-Broker Nyla reportedly observed that ☽'eocmoum's fire-core sales had increased — a detail whose implications Nyla would have understood better than most.
    
    ## The Ritus∴um and the Ledger
    
    The chronicle account states that during the Ritus∴um ceremony — which requires witnessing relics, speaking the names of the dead, and offering fire-core heat to the deep — ☽'eocmoum recognized that the ritual mirrored what the shard needed to reach the living. The shard compelled those who touched it to speak their names and drew them toward fatal heat.
    
    She chose to sell it. She opened the trade ledger and recorded the Exchange-assessed price: six season-equivalents, enough to sustain seventeen miners through the cold. She did not keep the shard. She did not walk away from it. She placed it back into the system that moves warmth and death through the colony in equal measure, and the record moved on without her.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
  annotation "note_1771593613095_0", anchor: "enough to sustain seventeen miners through the cold", text: "Seventeen. At the Hisper∴vi — the same festival where I flagged this number's recurrence in disaster accounts. Six season-equivalents converts to precisely seventeen miners' sustenance. Either Nightshelf pricing conventions round to seventeen by structural habit, or the number is load-bearing in ways I have failed, across forty-two annotation sessions, to identify. The ice at Foundation depth does not count. But something does.", type: :pedantic, display: :full
  annotation "note_1771593613095_1", anchor: "Shadow-Broker Nyla reportedly observed", text: "'Reportedly.' Nyla's trade-surveillance records are in Midnight Claws cipher, which I can barely read on a good day. The source for this detail is almost certainly secondhand — someone who heard Nyla say it, or someone who heard someone. Nyla herself confessed to murder and left no accessible operational archive. The observation rings true, which is precisely why it needs sourcing.", type: :skepticism, display: :popout
  annotation "note_1771593613095_2", anchor: "the record moved on without her", text: "It did. And now the record has circled back, because I put her in it. A merchant who held a dead miner's crystallized sacrifice in her warehouse, dreamed the dead woman's name, understood what the shard wanted, and chose neither heroism nor horror but commerce — the one system she trusted to carry dangerous things where they needed to go. She was right. The warmth reached the miners. And no one wrote down what happened to ☽'eocmoum after that, because no one was looking at her anymore. They were looking at the shard.", type: :commentary, display: :full
